1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,497
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,497 --> 00:00:08,830
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:15,125
QUALCOMM, ticker QCOM triggered and closed +1.5 percent above the entry.

4
00:00:15,125 --> 00:00:17,136
So far, so disciplined.

5
00:00:17,136 --> 00:00:22,072
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

6
00:00:22,072 --> 00:00:27,481
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

7
00:00:27,446 --> 00:00:31,110
On the day, the book is up 132 dollars.

8
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:34,116
Avnet (AVT) leads, +2.9 percent.

9
00:00:34,116 --> 00:00:37,028
Amazon.com (AMZN) lags at -1.3.

10
00:00:37,028 --> 00:00:42,101
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:42,101 --> 00:00:45,627
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

12
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:53,238
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

13
00:00:53,238 --> 00:01:02,929
12 positions, led by AbbVie, ticker ABBV, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing, ticker TSM, Arista Networks, ticker ANET.

14
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:14,004
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

15
00:01:13,958 --> 00:01:18,677
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

16
00:01:18,677 --> 00:01:21,036
Every morning OROS proposes.

17
00:01:21,036 --> 00:01:31,400
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

18
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:35,276
Inside the book today: 8 positions up, 4 down.

19
00:01:35,276 --> 00:01:37,551
Best, AVT at +2.90 percent.

20
00:01:37,551 --> 00:01:39,236
Worst, BGC at -1.45.

21
00:01:39,236 --> 00:01:44,123
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

22
00:01:44,123 --> 00:01:51,117
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.96% per trade over 979 graded positions.

23
00:01:51,117 --> 00:01:58,026
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

24
00:01:58,026 --> 00:02:03,082
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

25
00:02:03,082 --> 00:02:15,215
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 202 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

26
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:17,407
Over to the sports desk.

27
00:02:17,407 --> 00:02:26,650
Day 57 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

28
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:31,505
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 103,197.

29
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:38,414
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 2.4 thousand back at 100,843.

30
00:02:38,414 --> 00:02:45,603
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 5.5 thousand back at 97,648.

31
00:02:45,603 --> 00:02:48,217
The race has no finish line.

32
00:02:48,217 --> 00:02:52,512
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

33
00:02:52,512 --> 00:02:57,367
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

34
00:02:57,367 --> 00:02:59,235
Nothing is restated.

35
00:02:59,235 --> 00:03:08,142
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

36
00:03:08,104 --> 00:03:15,439
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

37
00:03:15,439 --> 00:03:17,681
Nothing here is financial advice.

38
00:03:17,681 --> 00:03:20,669
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
